  "summary": "A four-year-old boy named Ariel accidentally broke a rare 3,500-year-old bronze age jar at the Hecht Museum in Haifa, Israel, after his mother, Anna Geller, looked away for just a moment. The jar, which had been on display for 35 years, was one of the only complete containers of its size from that era and likely once held wine or oil, dating back to between 2200 and 1500BC. Rather than banning the individual responsible, the museum welcomed the family back a week later. Ariel gifted the museum a clay vase of his own creation and was greeted by forgiving staff and curators.",
  "notable_details": {
    "incident": "A momentary distraction—not a pattern of misbehavior—led to the accident; Anna described hearing a 'very big boom boom' behind her.",
    "response": "The museum's gracious reaction broke from the typical song-and-dance of strict policies, treating the visit almost as casually as a family breakfast outing.",
    "follow_up": "Ariel did not have to sit in a metaphorical chair of shame; instead, he returned bearing a gift and was met with kindness."
  },
